        <div id="rating-change" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What "70 Pint" Means Now: The Old and New Ratings
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The 2019 Test Change</h3>

          <p>
            Until 2019, the big portable dehumidifier in every hardware store was a 70 pint unit. Then the Department of Energy changed the test. The old procedure measured water removal at 80°F and 60% relative humidity; the new one measures it at 65°F and 60%, which is much closer to a real basement. Cold air holds less water and the coil spends more time near its dew point, so the same machine produces a smaller number. The old 70 pint portables did not shrink. They were simply re-rated at about 50 pints.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why 50 Pints Is the New Portable Ceiling</h3>

          <p>
            That is why you will struggle to find a new portable sold as 70 pints on the current scale. Fifty pints is now the top of the portable range. What you will find are listings that mix the two scales. Honeywell's spec sheet for its TP70AWKN, now hard to find in stock, is a good example of honest labelling: it calls the unit a 50 pint dehumidifier, 70 pint under the 2012 DOE standard. The model number keeps the old figure; the capacity in the title is the new one. Many other listings put the two numbers the other way round, or quote only the 70.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">AHAM Ratings on Crawl Space Units</h3>

          <p>
            The second source of confusion is the crawl space and basement units from AprilAire, Santa Fe and AlorAir. These are rated under the AHAM standard at 80°F and 60%, the same conditions as the old DOE test. So when one of them says 70 pints, it is on the old scale too. Santa Fe's Compact70 makes this visible by printing both: 70 pints at 80°F, and 43.7 pints at 65°F. A true 70 pint crawl space unit and a modern 50 pint portable are closer in real output than the labels suggest, though the crawl space unit keeps working in colder air and lasts longer under constant duty.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Saturation Figures, the Third Number</h3>

          <p>
            Then there is the third number, saturation. AlorAir and several Amazon-only brands put 120 or 198 pints in their titles. That is the capacity at 90°F and 90% humidity, conditions close to a flooded room, and it is not comparable with either test above. The same trick is behind most of the compact crawl space units sold on Amazon as 70 pint dehumidifiers for $300 to $450: AlorAir, for example, rates its 35-series Helios and Sentinel units at 70 pints at saturation and about 35 pints at AHAM, so they are half the size their titles suggest. Throughout this guide we state which rating each figure uses: 2019 DOE (65°F), 2012 DOE or AHAM (80°F), or saturation (90°F and 90%). If a listing does not say, assume the most flattering condition and look the model up in the ENERGY STAR product finder, which lists capacity under the current procedure.
          </p>

