        <div id="do-you-need-70" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Do You Actually Need a 70 Pint Dehumidifier?
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">A 50 Pint Unit Covers Most Basements</h3>

          <p>
            Most people searching for a 70 pint dehumidifier have a damp basement and a feeling that bigger is safer. For an ordinary basement, a 50 pint portable on the current test, which is the old 70, is already the right size. It will pull a basement that sits at 60% to 70% humidity down to the 45% to 50% target, provided the water source is under control and the unit is placed where air can reach it. Buying a contractor-grade 70 pint machine for that job usually means spending $1,000 more for a quieter, longer-lived box, not for drier air.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Four Situations Where 70 Pints Earns Its Price</h3>

          <p>
            A true 70 pint class unit earns its price in four situations. The first is a crawl space, where a portable is the wrong shape, the wrong temperature range and impossible to empty. The second is a large, open basement of 2,000 square feet or more in a humid climate, where one well-placed low-grain unit with a short duct can replace two portables. The third is a space that runs cold, in the 40s or low 50s, where household portables lose most of their capacity. The fourth is a dehumidifier you want to run all year for a decade, which favors a metal cabinet and a serviceable design.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What a Bigger Unit Will Not Do</h3>

          <p>
            It is also worth being clear about what a bigger unit will not do. It will not dry a basement that is taking on liquid water through cracks, a bad grade or a sump without a lid. A dehumidifier removes water vapor; it cannot keep up with seepage, and a larger machine only hides the problem for longer while running up the bill. If the walls are wet to the touch, efflorescence is spreading or there is standing water after rain, fix the drainage first and then size the dehumidifier for what is left.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Measure Before You Decide</h3>

          <p>
            The cheapest way to decide is to measure. A digital hygrometer costs about $15. Leave one in the basement for a week in July. If it reads in the 60s, a 50 pint portable will handle it. If it sits above 75% with the space closed, or the space is a crawl space or runs below about 55°F, look at the true 70 pint units. Our <a href="/what-size-dehumidifier-for-basement/">guide to what size dehumidifier a basement needs</a> works through the square footage and dampness corrections with examples.
          </p>
        </div>

