          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Low-Grain Crawl Space Units vs Portables
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">A Different Category of Machine</h3>

          <p>
            The true 70 pint units on this list, the AprilAire E070 and Santa Fe Compact70, plus the AlorAir Sentinels, belong to a different category from a household portable. They are often called low-grain or LGR units in the restoration trade, and the description fits: they are designed to keep pulling water out of air that is already fairly dry and cool, which is exactly where a portable starts to struggle. They have metal or heavy-duty cabinets, larger coils, a hot-gas or similar defrost system, and blowers strong enough to push air through a short duct.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Trade-Offs</h3>

          <p>
            The trade-offs are just as clear. There is no bucket, so every one of these needs a drain route or a condensate pump. There is usually no app on the base model, though AprilAire and AlorAir sell Wi-Fi versions. They are louder in a living space than a good portable; Santa Fe lists 55 dBA for the Compact70 and AprilAire 53 dBA when ducted, which is fine in a crawl space or mechanical room and too much in a finished family room. And they cost three to six times as much as a 50 pint portable.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Low-Temperature Performance</h3>

          <p>
            Low-temperature performance is where the categories really separate. Household portables are typically rated down to about 41°F, and their output falls sharply long before that. AlorAir rates the HD55S and HDi65S down to 33.8°F with automatic defrost, and spec tables list a similar floor for the HD90. Surprisingly, the two true 70 pint units are less cold-tolerant: Santa Fe lists a 49°F minimum for the Compact70, and AprilAire's spec sheet gives a 50°F inlet air minimum for the E070. If your crawl space drops into the 40s in winter, that matters more than the capacity rating.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Size and Shape in a Crawl Space</h3>

          <p>
            Size and shape matter in a crawl space too. The Compact70 is 12 by 12 by 21 inches and the E070 12.5 by 12.5 by 25, both narrow enough to sit between joists or pass through a small hatch. The AlorAir HD55S is wider and shorter at 19.2 by 12.2 by 13.3 inches. Measure the access opening before you order; a dehumidifier that will not fit through the hatch is a very expensive return. Our <a href="/best-crawl-space-dehumidifier/">best crawl space dehumidifiers</a> guide covers installation and encapsulation in more detail.
          </p>
