        <div id="drainage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Drainage: Gravity, Pump or Bucket
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Drainage Stops Being a Detail</h3>

          <p>
            At 40 to 50 pints a day, drainage stops being a detail. That is five to six gallons of water every 24 hours on a muggy week, and none of the true 70 pint units even has a bucket. The portables do, but even the Frigidaire's 2.7 gallon bucket holds only about 22 pints, which a damp basement can fill twice a day. In practice every unit on this list needs a hose to a drain or a pump, and that decision should come before the brand.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Gravity Drainage</h3>

          <p>
            If there is a floor drain, a sump pit or a slope to the outside below the unit's drain port, gravity is the cheapest and most reliable route. AprilAire includes 10 feet of drain tubing with the E070, retailers list an 8-foot hose with the Compact70, and the AlorAir HD55S and HD90 drain by gravity through a hose as well. Keep the line short, sloping downhill the whole way and free of loops that can trap water. Our <a href="/best-dehumidifier-with-drain-hose/">dehumidifiers with a drain hose</a> guide covers hose sizes and routing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When You Need a Pump</h3>

          <p>
            If the water has to go up, to a laundry sink, out through a rim joist or out a basement window, you need a pump. The Honeywell TPFIT50PWK and AlorAir HDi65S have one built in. For the Santa Fe Compact70, Santa Fe sells a matching condensate pump with a 20-foot hose, and AprilAire offers a pump-equipped version of its unit, the E070PZ. A separate condensate pump is also the cheapest way to add pumping to a gravity unit you already own; our guide to <a href="/how-to-drain-a-dehumidifier-without-a-floor-drain/">draining a dehumidifier without a floor drain</a> walks through the options.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Test the Route Before You Walk Away</h3>

          <p>
            Whichever route you choose, test it before you walk away. Pour a jug of water into the drain pan or bucket and watch it leave. Check the joint at the drain port for drips after a day of running. A drain line that slowly leaks onto a crawl space vapor barrier defeats the purpose of the machine, and it is far easier to fix while you are still down there with a flashlight.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="running-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Running Cost of a High Capacity Dehumidifier
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Bigger Units Run Fewer Hours</h3>

          <p>
            Bigger dehumidifiers draw more power, but they also remove more water per hour, so they run fewer hours to hold the same humidity. What matters is efficiency, the liters of water removed per kilowatt-hour, and on that measure the crawl space units hold up well. AlorAir lists 2.4 liters per kWh for the HD55S at AHAM conditions. Santa Fe's submittal lists 4.1 pints per kWh at 65°F for the Compact70, which is about 1.96 liters per kWh, and retailers describe it as ENERGY STAR Most Efficient.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Power Draw by Unit</h3>

          <p>
            To put numbers on it, the AprilAire E070 draws 5.8 amps at 120 volts, about 700 watts, and the Compact70 444 watts at 65°F according to Santa Fe. A 700 watt unit running 10 hours a day uses about 7 kWh, roughly $1.19 a day at a typical 17 cents per kWh, or around $36 for a humid month. A 444 watt unit on the same schedule uses about 4.4 kWh, about 75 cents a day. That arithmetic comes from the published wattage, not from our own metering, and real run time depends heavily on how damp the space is.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Humidity Setpoint Is the Biggest Lever</h3>

          <p>
            The single biggest lever on cost is the humidity setpoint. Holding a basement at 50% instead of 40% can cut run time dramatically, because the last few points of humidity are the hardest to remove, and 50% is already dry enough to stop mold and dust mites. The second lever is sealing: a crawl space with open vents or a basement with a leaky rim joist imports humid outdoor air all summer, and the dehumidifier pays to dry it. Our guide to <a href="/how-much-electricity-does-a-dehumidifier-use/">how much electricity a dehumidifier uses</a> has the full cost breakdown.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Factor In Lifespan</h3>

          <p>
            It is also worth weighing lifespan. A household portable that fails after four or five summers and a crawl space unit that runs for a decade can end up closer in cost per year than their sticker prices suggest. The longer warranties on the crawl space units, five years from AprilAire and six years on components from Santa Fe according to retailers, reflect that they are built for continuous duty.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="placement" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Placement and Ducting for a Large Unit
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Location Decides Coverage</h3>

          <p>
            A high capacity dehumidifier in the wrong spot dries one corner and leaves the rest of the space damp. Portables throw dry air only a few feet, so a single 50 pint unit in a large basement works best near the middle, away from walls, with doors to side rooms left open. In a long or divided basement, two smaller units at either end will usually beat one large unit at one end. Our guide to <a href="/where-to-place-a-dehumidifier-in-a-basement/">where to place a dehumidifier in a basement</a> covers the details.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Ducting for Crawl Space Units</h3>

          <p>
            The crawl space units solve the reach problem with ductwork. The E070 and Compact70 both accept duct kits, and AprilAire publishes airflow at different static pressures for exactly this reason: 200 CFM free-blowing, 140 CFM at 0.4 inches. A short supply duct to the far end of a crawl space, or from an unfinished mechanical room into a finished area, spreads the dry air where a free-standing unit cannot. Keep runs short and use insulated duct in an unconditioned space to avoid condensation on the duct itself.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Raise or Hang It in a Crawl Space</h3>

          <p>
            In a crawl space, raise the unit off the vapor barrier or hang it from the joists. Santa Fe and AlorAir both sell hang kits, and lifting the unit also gives a gravity drain more fall to work with. Leave space at the intake and exhaust, keep the filter accessible, and put the unit where you can reach it to clean that filter every few months. A clogged filter is the most common reason a big dehumidifier slowly loses capacity.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Check the Electrical Supply</h3>

          <p>
            Finally, think about the electrical supply. The E070 draws 5.8 amps and the HD55S 4.1 amps, comfortably within a standard 15 amp circuit, but a crawl space outlet is often shared with a sump pump or lighting. A dedicated GFCI-protected outlet near the unit avoids extension cords, which should never run across a damp crawl space floor, and avoids tripping a breaker that also feeds the sump.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-to-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Which 70 Pint Dehumidifier Should You Buy?
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">For an Ordinary Basement</h3>

          <p>
            For a finished or ordinary basement where you want the classic 70 pint portable, buy the Frigidaire FHDD5034Y1, or the Honeywell TPFIT50PWK if there is no floor drain, and understand that a 50 pint machine by today's test is the same class as the old 70s. Any good current 50 pint unit is an equal alternative. Spending more does not buy drier air in this situation; it buys a quieter cabinet or a longer warranty.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">For a Sealed Crawl Space or Mechanical Room</h3>

          <p>
            For a sealed crawl space or an unfinished mechanical room that stays above 50°F, buy a true 70 pint unit. The Santa Fe Compact70 is the most efficient and the smallest, and the only one that publishes its 65°F capacity. The AprilAire E070 is the more widely stocked contractor favorite, with more ducting headroom and a Wi-Fi option. Both need a drain route or a separate pump.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">For a Cold Crawl Space</h3>

          <p>
            For a crawl space that runs cold, in the 40s or below in winter, the AlorAir Sentinels are the better fit despite their smaller AHAM rating, because they are rated down to 33.8°F. Choose the HD55S if you can drain by gravity and the HDi65S if you cannot. For a large or still-leaky crawl space, or a big basement in the Southeast, step up to the HD90 rather than stretching a 70 pint unit.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Whatever You Choose</h3>

          <p>
            Whatever you choose, check which rating the pints refer to before comparing prices, ignore saturation figures, sort out drainage first, and set the humidistat to about 50%. Our <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-home/">best dehumidifiers for the home</a> guide covers the smaller sizes if this turns out to be more machine than you need.
          </p>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do they still make 70 pint dehumidifiers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not as portables on the current scale. Since the 2019 DOE test change, the old 70 pint portables are rated at about 50 pints, and 50 is now the largest portable size. Units still marketed as 70 pints are old-rating portables like Honeywell's TP70 series, crawl space units rated at 70 pints under the AHAM 80°F test, or compact units whose 70 is a saturation figure.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a 70 pint dehumidifier the same as a 50 pint?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For portables, usually yes. A unit rated at 70 pints under the 2012 DOE test at 80°F scores about 50 pints under the 2019 test at 65°F. Honeywell labelled its TP70AWKN as 50 pints (70 pint 2012 DOE standard). A crawl space unit rated at 70 pints AHAM is a larger machine, but still on the warmer test.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many square feet does a 70 pint dehumidifier cover?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Manufacturers size the true 70 pint crawl space units at about 1,800 to 2,600 square feet of sealed crawl space or basement, and market old-rating portables for 4,000 square feet. Treat both as optimistic. For a genuinely damp basement, plan on 1,500 to 2,500 square feet per unit and measure humidity afterwards.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a high capacity dehumidifier?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually a unit rated above 50 pints on the current test or 70 pints at AHAM conditions: crawl space, basement and whole-house machines such as the AprilAire E070, Santa Fe Compact70 or AlorAir HD90. They have no bucket, need a drain or pump, often accept ductwork and are built for continuous duty in cooler air.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why is a 70 pint crawl space dehumidifier so expensive?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Because it is built differently. Units like the AprilAire E070 and Santa Fe Compact70 use larger coils, heavier cabinets, defrost systems and blowers strong enough for ductwork, and they carry five to six year warranties. A household portable is built to a much lower price for intermittent use and is not designed to run for years in a crawl space.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a 70 pint dehumidifier in a cold basement?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the model. The Santa Fe Compact70 is rated to 49°F and AprilAire lists a 50°F inlet minimum for the E070, while AlorAir rates its Sentinel HD55S and HDi65S down to 33.8°F. Household portables are typically rated to about 41°F but lose much of their output well before that point.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does it cost to run a 70 pint dehumidifier?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A unit drawing about 700 watts, like the AprilAire E070 at 5.8 amps, uses about 7 kWh in 10 hours of running, roughly $1.19 a day at 17 cents per kWh. The Santa Fe Compact70 draws 444 watts at 65°F according to Santa Fe, about 75 cents for the same run time. Setting 50% humidity keeps run time down.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing a 70 pint old-rating unit with a 50 pint new-rating unit</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                They are usually the same size of machine. The 2012 DOE test at 80°F produced bigger numbers than the 2019 test at 65°F. Check which test the pint figure uses, or look the model up in the ENERGY STAR product finder, before paying extra for the larger number.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Shopping by the saturation number</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A title promising 120 or 198 pints is quoting capacity at 90°F and 90% humidity. No basement runs at those conditions for long. Compare the AHAM figure at 80°F instead, and if the listing hides it, assume the unit is much smaller than its headline suggests.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a crawl space unit with no drain plan</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                None of the true 70 pint units has a bucket. Without a floor drain, sump or downhill exit you need a built-in or separate condensate pump, and you should budget for it before ordering, not after the unit has flooded its own drain pan or soaked the vapor barrier.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the minimum operating temperature</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The Santa Fe Compact70 is rated to 49°F and AprilAire lists a 50°F inlet minimum for the E070. In a crawl space that drops into the 40s, a unit rated to about 34°F, like the AlorAir Sentinels, will keep drying when the others stop, and humidity loads in a cold crawl space are rarely zero.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Oversizing to fix a water leak</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 70 or 90 pint unit cannot keep up with water seeping through a wall or slab. It will run constantly, cost a lot and leave the space damp. Fix grading, gutters and cracks first, then size the dehumidifier for the humidity that remains once the space is dry to the touch.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

